OpenStax's source MathML for exercise 23's domain specification uses a bare
<mo>ϵ</mo> (U+03F5 GREEK LUNATE EPSILON SYMBOL) where "element of" (∈, U+2208)
is clearly intended ("the domain x ∈ [-3,3]") -- confirmed against the raw
fetched page content, appearing in both the presentation and content MathML
for this one exercise and nowhere else on the page. This is a one-off
mistake in OpenStax's own equation editor, not a MathML-tag/symbol-mapping
gap (the converter faithfully reproduces \epsilon for that exact character
elsewhere), so the fix is restored by hand here rather than in the
converter.

Manual review pass: the graphed-f' figure descriptions left bare-text
"f'(x)", "x axis"/"x-axis", and coordinate pairs like "(-2, 0)" outside math
mode; these are now typeset as $f'(x)$, $x$-axis, and $(-2,0)$ per this
repo's established convention (see e.g. vol2-2-1-areas-between-curves.tex,
vol2-3-1-integration-by-parts.tex). One figure description also mistyped
$x^{3}$ as bare "x3". The sin(x)/x domain exercise had lost its minus sign
in conversion -- $[2\pi,0)$ is not a valid interval (lower bound exceeds
upper bound) -- and is restored to $[-2\pi,0)$, matching the well-known
symmetric-about-the-origin domain for this exercise; the two adjacent math
spans "$x=$ $[...]$" are also joined into one, matching the file's own
convention elsewhere (e.g. "over $x=[-1,1]$"). Finally, "f,f',\,\text{and}\,
f''" is rewritten as "$f$, $f'$, and $f''$" to keep the English "and"
outside math mode rather than joined by \text and manual spacing.

